It can look quite different under different style
sheets, too, but that's a feature. The difference
between browsers is mostly due to 1) places where
the HTML 4.01 standard deliberately leaves things
implementation dependent, to make finalizing a
standard possible at all [I worked for about 8 years
on an American National Standards technical
committee; getting anything out of a standards
committee at all is a series of political
compromises, and "leave it up to the
users/implementors" when the standards committee is
unable to reach agreement on what a standard should
standardize is one such compromise frequently made],
and 2) incompetence of browser designers and
implementers to follow what the standard says,
or deliberate violation of standards to lock users
into ones product, Microsoft the most egregious such
offender in both cases.

>> Others here have recommended tools in the fairly
>> ancient (1980s) [ngt]roff family, which can produce
>> quite pretty output "eventually", but that uses
>> incredibly ugly markup compared to HTML.

> Yes, it's ancient, indeed. (Whether HTML has
> prettier syntax - hmm, maybe.)

No, really. HTML is an instance of SGML, and
everything except the specific "<" ">" tagging used
for markup, and perhaps the "&...;" character glyph
entity encodings, are going to be perfectly familiar
already to new web page programmers, which is why
"teaching yourself HTML coding" works so well. The
*roff markup in contrast is 1) a hodgepodge of
different markups catering to the many processors
that can be put in the *roff pipeline, 2) a markup
nesting nightmare, and 3) nonintuitive as heck since
pretty much none of it is base on prior art familiar
to markup coders today.

Moreover, one _wants_ to program directly in HTML
markup; the markup produced by automatic web page
writing tools is so incredibly hideous it is write
only, one cannot read it intelligently to find and
repair a problem.

However, as said above, I should have checked my
work, since what the OP seemed to want to produce
was "a fixed pitch text _book_", and it turns out
that HTML lacks the concept of pagination, making
creation of a book much too hard a chore, since it
would need to be done one page at a time. Bleah.

Pagination _can_ be done in web pages by non-HTML
scripting like use of Javascript or PHP, but
even those who do paginate that way describe it
as an ugly kludge.

